> API and interpretation

The original base10/mod101 illustration remapped letters to1,2 in a separate
report hash. The new figure uses actual native unsigned raw bytes97,98 under
those same configurable hash parameters, so the numerical hashes intentionally
differ while exact offsets remain0,2. The normal public search retains
base256/mod1000000007. The tiny collision example has both c and a congruent
modulo2 yet rejects c by exact bytes. Counters count actual scalar verification
work, not optimized string-library instructions. The repeated-pattern control
has genuine matches; it shows O(m) verification at each window without claiming
they are spurious collisions.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

